[resource]
script = ExtResource("1_page")
id = &"economy"
title = "Resources and gathering"
order = 80
summary = "Four resources, why the walk back costs you, and the one you can grow."
body = "Four resources: FOOD, WOOD, GOLD and STONE. What each thing costs is on the
Troops and Buildings pages.

Three of the four are FOUND and never replaced. Food is the exception: it is
also GROWN, and that is what keeps a long match from ending in an empty map.

Gathering is a ROUND TRIP. A crew walks to a node, works it, fills up, walks
back to a drop-off and unloads. So distance is income: a node across the map
earns a fraction of what the same node beside your town does, and the
difference is the walk, every trip, all match.

A storehouse is a drop-off you can put anywhere. It is usually far cheaper
than moving your economy to the resources.

## Nodes run out

Trees hold little and go quickly; gold and stone last a long time. When a
crew works a tree out it retargets itself to the nearest surviving node of
the SAME kind close by — it will never switch you from wood to food behind
your back. When nothing of that kind is left in reach it stops, and a stopped
crew is waiting for orders.

Wood never comes back. Plan on the map running down, because it does.

Gold and stone are the exception, and only just. A worked-out vein is not
dead: it regrows, slowly and permanently, toward a small tail — 150 gold or
240 stone, about ten minutes' worth. That is a FLOOR, not a replacement. A
live seam pays a crew several times faster and holds 2,400, so the opening is
still a race for rich ground; a regrown vein buffers a raid or a rebuild and
nothing more.

## A farm is a node you built

A FARM is a work site you put where you want one, and a crew is ordered onto
it exactly as onto a forest. The difference is the direction it moves: a
field's stock grows back instead of only running down, so a farm is a RATE
and a forest is a pile.

Two things follow. The only way to raise a rate is to own more fields, so
farms are the one building you want several of. And a field is worth nothing
standing empty — an unworked farm feeds nobody, so each one wants a crew.

A farm does not block movement and does not defend itself, which makes a
field the softest thing you own. Raiding somebody's fields costs them the
income, not just the harvest sitting in them.

## The ground says what grows on it

Forests sit where it is wet, orchards through the middle, dry trees and palms
on sand, and stone at the foot of mountains. Which means you can read where
somebody's resources must be from terrain you have only glimpsed.

## Casualties cut income

A crew gathers per LIVING soldier, and each worker carries his own share of
the crew's load — a half-strength crew fills half a load, and men killed on
the walk home drop what they carried. Raiding gatherers is not only denial
while you are standing there — it is a permanent cut to their rate until the
men are replaced, and their packs fall with them."
sources = Array[String](["res://economy.gd", "res://decisions/D-027.md", "res://decisions/D-087-forests-are-made-of-trees-biome.md", "res://buildings/storehouse.tres", "res://buildings/farm.tres", "res://decisions/D-20260828-food-is-grown-not-only-found.md"])
stamp = "a345e0f145eee4f7116363325ca21bd548a00d91aaf25e24487b721ab25819ed"
